1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,980
A sponge in the sea.

2
00:00:01,980 --> 00:00:03,757
A jellyfish on the beach.

3
00:00:03,757 --> 00:00:05,426
An earthworm after rain.

4
00:00:05,426 --> 00:00:06,906
A crow on the wire.

5
00:00:07,306 --> 00:00:11,867
This chapter gave us eleven phyla,
and then whole classes of vertebrates.

6
00:00:11,867 --> 00:00:14,060
Too many to remember one by one.

7
00:00:14,510 --> 00:00:19,116
Aarav likes machines, so he asked:
can one flowchart sort every animal,

8
00:00:19,116 --> 00:00:21,329
with a few yes or no questions?

9
00:00:21,679 --> 00:00:22,579
It can.

10
00:00:22,439 --> 00:00:27,652
The basis of classification in NCERT
gives us the questions, one at a time.

11
00:00:28,152 --> 00:00:32,231
By the end, you will build the whole
key yourself, and walk any animal

12
00:00:32,231 --> 00:00:34,248
down it to its phylum and class.

13
00:00:34,898 --> 00:00:38,681
The key asks only four questions,
always in the same order.

14
00:00:39,031 --> 00:00:39,931
One.

15
00:00:39,525 --> 00:00:41,415
How are the cells organised?

16
00:00:41,415 --> 00:00:42,315
Two.

17
00:00:41,909 --> 00:00:43,701
What is the body symmetry?

18
00:00:44,151 --> 00:00:45,051
Three.

19
00:00:44,773 --> 00:00:47,557
Is there a body cavity, and what kind?

20
00:00:47,557 --> 00:00:48,457
Four.

21
00:00:48,128 --> 00:00:49,698
Is there a notochord?

22
00:00:49,698 --> 00:00:52,736
Each answer sends the
animal down one branch.

23
00:00:53,386 --> 00:00:55,816
Let's build the key, branch by branch.

24
00:00:56,166 --> 00:00:57,066
Step one.

25
00:00:56,982 --> 00:01:00,806
Cells only loosely working
together, at the cellular level?

26
00:01:00,806 --> 00:01:02,947
That is Porifera, the sponges.

27
00:01:02,947 --> 00:01:06,313
Everyone else has tissues,
organs or organ systems.

28
00:01:07,063 --> 00:01:07,986
Step two.

29
00:01:07,986 --> 00:01:10,924
Radial symmetry, with two germ layers?

30
00:01:10,924 --> 00:01:15,404
Those are Coelenterata, also
called Cnidaria, and Ctenophora.

31
00:01:16,154 --> 00:01:17,107
Step three.

32
00:01:17,107 --> 00:01:20,070
Bilateral symmetry,
with three germ layers.

33
00:01:20,070 --> 00:01:21,986
Now check the body cavity.

34
00:01:21,986 --> 00:01:25,425
No cavity at all:
Platyhelminthes, the flatworms.

35
00:01:26,175 --> 00:01:30,759
A false cavity, not lined by mesoderm:
Aschelminthes, the roundworms.

36
00:01:31,509 --> 00:01:36,629
A true coelom: Annelida, Arthropoda,
Mollusca, Echinodermata, Hemichordata

37
00:01:36,629 --> 00:01:37,733
and Chordata.

38
00:01:38,483 --> 00:01:39,472
Step four.

39
00:01:39,472 --> 00:01:40,574
A notochord?

40
00:01:40,574 --> 00:01:41,790
Only Chordata.

41
00:01:41,790 --> 00:01:46,880
Then jaws split the vertebrates, and
fins or limbs split the jawed ones

42
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:50,028
into fishes and four classes of tetrapods.

43
00:01:50,678 --> 00:01:52,190
Now watch the key work.

44
00:01:52,190 --> 00:01:54,210
Drop an earthworm in at the top.

45
00:01:54,560 --> 00:01:57,921
Organ system level, so
it goes past the sponges.

46
00:01:57,921 --> 00:02:00,005
Bilateral, three germ layers.

47
00:02:00,455 --> 00:02:02,201
It has a true coelom.

48
00:02:02,201 --> 00:02:03,314
No notochord.

49
00:02:03,314 --> 00:02:04,995
It lands on Annelida.

50
00:02:05,445 --> 00:02:06,819
Now drop a crow.

51
00:02:06,819 --> 00:02:10,174
Coelomate, notochord,
jaws, limbs, feathers.

52
00:02:10,174 --> 00:02:11,602
It lands on Aves.

53
00:02:12,252 --> 00:02:15,534
Two radial animals that
sit on different branches.

54
00:02:15,534 --> 00:02:17,465
A jellyfish and a starfish.

55
00:02:17,815 --> 00:02:20,166
The jellyfish has two germ layers.

56
00:02:20,166 --> 00:02:21,835
The starfish has three.

57
00:02:22,285 --> 00:02:24,202
The jellyfish has no coelom.

58
00:02:24,202 --> 00:02:26,325
The starfish has a true coelom.

59
00:02:26,775 --> 00:02:29,354
And only the adult starfish is radial.

60
00:02:29,354 --> 00:02:31,000
Its larva is bilateral.

61
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:33,789
So the key places it with the coelomates.

62
00:02:34,439 --> 00:02:35,339
Your turn.

63
00:02:35,327 --> 00:02:39,389
A leech has a true coelom,
and its body is made of rings.

64
00:02:39,389 --> 00:02:40,430
Which phylum?

65
00:02:40,430 --> 00:02:41,736
Pause and decide.

66
00:02:44,936 --> 00:02:45,836
Annelida!

67
00:02:45,744 --> 00:02:50,977
A true coelom, bilateral, no notochord,
and a body divided into segments.

68
00:02:50,977 --> 00:02:53,434
The leech is Hirudinaria in NCERT.

69
00:02:54,084 --> 00:02:55,130
Remember this.

70
00:02:55,130 --> 00:02:57,613
Cells, symmetry, cavity, notochord.

71
00:02:57,613 --> 00:03:00,917
Four questions, in that
order, sort every animal.

72
00:03:01,517 --> 00:03:03,181
Now, a worked example.

73
00:03:03,181 --> 00:03:05,613
Let's walk four animals down the key.

74
00:03:05,963 --> 00:03:06,863
Sycon.

75
00:03:06,698 --> 00:03:09,156
Cellular level of organisation.

76
00:03:09,156 --> 00:03:10,071
Porifera.

77
00:03:10,522 --> 00:03:12,159
Taenia, the tapeworm.

78
00:03:12,159 --> 00:03:15,392
Bilateral, three germ
layers, no body cavity.

79
00:03:15,392 --> 00:03:16,542
Platyhelminthes.

80
00:03:16,992 --> 00:03:18,705
Ascaris, the roundworm.

81
00:03:18,705 --> 00:03:20,947
Bilateral, with a false cavity.

82
00:03:20,947 --> 00:03:21,977
Aschelminthes.

83
00:03:22,427 --> 00:03:23,985
Columba, the pigeon.

84
00:03:23,985 --> 00:03:27,469
True coelom, notochord,
jaws, limbs and feathers.

85
00:03:27,469 --> 00:03:28,369
Aves.

86
00:03:28,689 --> 00:03:30,155
Now, the exam trap.

87
00:03:30,505 --> 00:03:34,712
Many students put starfish with
jellyfish, because both look radial.

88
00:03:35,112 --> 00:03:39,573
But echinoderms are triploblastic
and coelomate, with bilateral larvae.

89
00:03:39,573 --> 00:03:41,844
They sit on the coelomate branch.

90
00:03:42,494 --> 00:03:43,893
Where is this asked?

91
00:03:43,893 --> 00:03:48,196
As statements on the basis of
classification, and placing a phylum on the

92
00:03:48,196 --> 00:03:49,155
right branch.

93
00:03:49,755 --> 00:03:50,953
Exam level now.

94
00:03:50,953 --> 00:03:53,615
Read the four statements about the key.

95
00:03:53,615 --> 00:03:55,281
Which ones are correct?

96
00:03:55,281 --> 00:03:56,531
Pause and solve.

97
00:04:01,931 --> 00:04:03,220
The answer is D.

98
00:04:03,220 --> 00:04:05,420
Statements one, three and four.

99
00:04:05,920 --> 00:04:07,110
Two is wrong.

100
00:04:07,110 --> 00:04:11,385
Ctenophores are radial and
diploblastic, like the cnidarians.

101
00:04:11,885 --> 00:04:13,369
One is the sponges.

102
00:04:13,369 --> 00:04:15,110
Three is the roundworms.

103
00:04:15,110 --> 00:04:19,222
Four is Hemichordata: coelomate,
but with no true notochord.

104
00:04:19,822 --> 00:04:21,283
Here is your recap.

105
00:04:21,283 --> 00:04:22,784
Screenshot this card.

106
00:04:25,984 --> 00:04:28,821
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

107
00:04:29,221 --> 00:04:30,121
One.

108
00:04:29,653 --> 00:04:32,534
What are the four questions
of the key, in order?

109
00:04:33,434 --> 00:04:34,334
Two.

110
00:04:33,957 --> 00:04:37,333
Why is the starfish not
placed with the jellyfish?

111
00:04:38,233 --> 00:04:39,133
Three.

112
00:04:38,811 --> 00:04:40,573
Walk a frog down the key.

113
00:04:40,573 --> 00:04:41,937
Where does it land?

114
00:04:43,037 --> 00:04:47,821
That completes Diversity in the
Living World: all four chapters done.

115
00:04:47,821 --> 00:04:51,125
Find every episode in
the PUC Biology playlist.
